-- NEGATIVE fixture for the rule-date interval table (spec §15.3, R10).
--
-- A MIXED chain: arms 1 and 3 are rule-date guards, arm 2 is an ordinary
-- boolean. There is no single date axis to put on a column, so there is no
-- honest interval table -- and half-converting it would be worse than either
-- alternative, because a chain that mixes a law-time arm with an ordinary one
-- is exactly how a temporal bug hides. The recogniser is therefore
-- ALL-OR-NOTHING (the shape "L4.OpenFisca.Lower"'s `splitDated` uses), and this
-- is the arm of that rule with no other witness in the tree: the corpus's
-- `financial statements required` matches ZERO rows and so is `NotDated`, which
-- is a different code path with a different outcome.
--
-- Expected: one D-DATEDCHAIN (Blocking) naming arm 2, and the sound
-- boolean-column fallback still ships.
--
-- NEVER added to `goldenSubjects`. Asserted from jl4/tests/DmnExport.hs.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

§ `mixed dated chain`

TIMEZONE IS "Asia/Singapore"

GIVETH A DATE
`the 2024 rate change` MEANS Date 1 1 2024

GIVETH A DATE
`GST commenced` MEANS Date 1 4 1994

ASSUME `no GST rate exists before commencement` IS A NUMBER

ASSUME `the transitional relief applies` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN change IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the rules in force include` change MEANS
    `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE` AT LEAST change

GIVETH A NUMBER
`GST rate percent` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the rules in force include` `the 2024 rate change` THEN 9
           IF `the transitional relief applies`                   THEN 0
           IF `the rules in force include` `GST commenced`        THEN 3
           OTHERWISE `no GST rate exists before commencement`
